      <!-- SECTION 5: SAFETY & REGULATIONS -->
      <section class="section" id="safety-regulatory">
        <h2>5. Safety and Regulatory Compliance</h2>
        <p>Aerosol manufacturing is classified as a high-risk industrial activity due to the combined hazards of flammable gases, pressurized containers, and volatile solvents. As a result, aerosol plants are subject to strict safety and regulatory frameworks. Designing a compliant facility requires specialized engineering controls to prevent vapor accumulation and eliminate ignition sources in production zones.</p>

        <div class="image-slot">
          <img 
            src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/AGBzPMBJDQiwDGny/a_basic_factory_floor_view_showing_a-5JmiyIANCLp4jvOr.jpeg" 
            alt="Factory floor view of an aerosol filling line inside a manufacturing plant" 
            loading="lazy" 
            width="800" 
            height="450" 
          />
        </div>
        <p class="image-caption" style="text-align: center; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: -1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; font-style: italic;">
          Figure 4: Basic aerosol filling and conveying area inside a manufacturing plant.
        </p>

        <h3>Essential Safety Protocols in Aerosol Manufacturing</h3>
        <p>Operating an aerosol plant requires strict adherence to international safety standards to manage flammability and high-pressure hazards. High-speed venting systems, hazardous area zoning, gas detection loops, and grounding systems are essential components of a compliant plant layout. Below are the core safety protocols that must be integrated into the facility design:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Hazardous area zoning and explosion-proof electrical systems</strong>: Classifying production areas (such as gassing rooms) according to ATEX or IECEx regulations to ensure only explosion-proof, spark-free machinery is operated in high-risk zones.</li>
          <li><strong>Gas detection and automatic emergency shut-off</strong>: Installing continuous gas sensors at floor level (as hydrocarbon propellants are heavier than air) to trigger automatic emergency gas shut-offs if concentrations exceed safe thresholds.</li>
          <li><strong>Grounding and bonding against static electricity</strong>: Implementing complete electrical grounding and bonding of all metal pipelines, conveying machinery, and containers to eliminate static discharge hazards.</li>
          <li><strong>Foam-based and dry chemical fire suppression</strong>: Deploying dedicated foam-based deluge and dry chemical fire suppression systems designed to quickly contain and extinguish propellant-fed fires.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Navigating Global Regulations</h3>
        <p>Modern compliance frameworks include UN and DOT transport rules, GHS labeling, VOC emission limits, and national fire and explosives regulations. Regulations evolved after the phase-out of CFC propellants under the Montreal Protocol and continue to emphasize environmental and occupational safety.</p>

        <h3>Pressure Testing and Container Integrity Requirements</h3>
        <p>Every manufactured aerosol canister must meet rigorous pressure resistance and leak-prevention criteria before being shipped. Regulatory bodies require continuous testing during production to verify that containers can handle high thermal stresses and rough transport handling. The following testing methods are standard for verifying container integrity:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Hydrostatic burst testing of containers</strong>: Destructively testing random container samples to verify that the metal walls and seams can withstand pressures well beyond standard operating limits.</li>
          <li><strong>Pressure retention testing over shelf life</strong>: Monitoring statistical samples over extended periods in temperature-controlled chambers to measure long-term gas and pressure loss.</li>
          <li><strong>Leak testing using hot water bath and gas sniffers</strong>: Passing 100% of filled canisters through a hot water bath (at 50°C) to expand the gas, checking for bubbles that indicate valve or crimp leaks.</li>
          <li><strong>Drop and mechanical impact testing</strong>: Subjecting filled aerosol cans to drop tests from set heights to confirm that the container seams do not rupture under impact.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6: FROM LAB TO MARKET -->
      <section class="section" id="lab-to-market">
        <h2>6. From Lab to Market: Initial Steps for Entrepreneurs</h2>
        <p>Bringing a new aerosol product to market involves balancing formulation chemistry, propellant selection, and mechanical facility planning. Because the initial capital requirement for specialized filling equipment is high, early-stage brands must carefully weigh their entry strategy. By starting with outsourced production, startups can validate their product in the market before investing in an in-house manufacturing plant.</p>

